WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:10.970
My name is Peter Jessen.

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:13.840
I'm a saxophone restorer and maker.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.300
I'm situated in Copenhagen in Denmark.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.060
I'll walk you through how I restore

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:21.740
a dented 1927 soprano saxophone.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.710
This saxophone is totally
out of working order.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:26.080
It's badly dented.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.130
It's bent.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:28.490
I'll have to take it all apart,

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:30.920
and a lot of it is rusted together.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.245
A lot of dent has to be taken out.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.550
In order to make a good job
on a saxophone like this,

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:42.473
you have to take everything apart.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:46.270
Every part has to come off,

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:48.800
because you can't really work on bodywork

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.010
with all the keywork on it.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:52.818
With the keywork rusted,

00:00:52.818 --> 00:00:54.110
everything has to be addressed.

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:56.526
So you strip it completely,

00:00:56.526 --> 00:00:58.259
take all keywork off.

00:01:00.316 --> 00:01:03.453
For the disassembly, I use standard tools:

00:01:03.453 --> 00:01:07.248
screwdrivers, pliers,

00:01:07.248 --> 00:01:10.403
a little Bunsen burner
to heat up, and oils.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:17.250
The springs on old
instruments are often useless.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:20.500
They have lost all their
flexibility and strength,

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.470
so they have to be clipped
off or pushed out or both.

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:25.220
And sometimes it can be really taxing,

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:28.100
because they also are subject to rusting.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:29.920
In order to get a perfect
functioning instrument,

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.661
you have to replace all the springs

00:01:31.661 --> 00:01:33.244
to get it really nice.

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:36.720
Here, I am removing the thumb hook,

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.030
which is what supports your right hand

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.630
when you carry the
saxophone and you play it.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:43.470
This is a small hook that's

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.900
soldered onto the tube for support.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.510
And as it sticks out, it is often the one

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:50.640
that gets dented into the instrument.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.980
The hook itself is quite thick and hard,

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.570
and you have to remove
it to get to the tube

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:57.462
to get the dent out properly.

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:05.930
I have different tapered mantle parts

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:08.070
that I can put on a threaded rod,

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:09.940
so they will fit the exact spot

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:11.850
inside the bore of the instrument.

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:14.260
And I can push out the dents with that

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:16.450
and make a pretty good result

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:19.670
rather than using a ball
or something not accurate.

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:21.340
This is a very good method of

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.063
getting the dents out precisely.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:27.208
Problem with the instrument and dents

00:02:27.208 --> 00:02:30.727
is very much where they
are and how big they are.

00:02:30.727 --> 00:02:32.550
'Cause a lot of players play on

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.560
fairly dented instruments with no problem.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.860
If they're underneath posts
that support mechanism,

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:39.700
you get problems.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.184
Particularly on a small instrument
like a soprano saxophone,

00:02:42.184 --> 00:02:45.870
it is essential, because
the tubing is very narrow

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:49.573
and you can't afford much
discrepancy for the airflow.

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:53.820
Certain areas of the instrument

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.680
I can't get to with the
tapered mantle parts.

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:58.870
I have to use a dent ball.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:00.700
And I measure up the dent ball

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:03.250
to fit inside the tube where the dent is.

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:04.647
I slid it in.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:15.865
And after that, I slid
on the next ball dent,

00:03:15.865 --> 00:03:17.710
and I can use that as a hammer

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:20.220
by swinging the instrument up and down.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:22.860
So I push the first dent ball

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:26.233
through where the dent is
and remove it that way.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.420
Here I am using a hand-roller burnisher

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:33.970
to smooth the surface,

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:35.940
because after the dent balls

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:38.580
there will be slight
ripples on the surface,

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:41.830
and you can remove that and
also round the instruments.

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:45.223
If it's got a few high
points, you can lower them.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:50.837
The critical thing about dent work

00:03:50.837 --> 00:03:54.000
is that you cannot expand
the bore of the instrument,

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.028
which is an easy thing

00:03:55.028 --> 00:03:57.486
if you slam on with a hammer

00:03:57.486 --> 00:03:59.027
or you push too hard

00:03:59.027 --> 00:04:00.593
with a dent tool or something,

00:04:00.593 --> 00:04:03.890
because expansion of the body
tube will disturb the bore,

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:05.760
which is essential for tuning,

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.320
which will affect your playing

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:09.528
and the instrument's playability.

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:16.620
This saxophone is very old.

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:18.860
It's been lying around
probably for decades,

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:21.820
and silver plate will tarnish with age.

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:25.900
So it's very, very dull,
gray-black-looking,

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:27.940
and it's such a nice
silver finish underneath.

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:29.540
So the effort of polishing

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:31.590
will make an instrument that's beautiful.

00:04:32.503 --> 00:04:35.640
On bigger instruments, you
might use a polishing machine.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:37.030
I could use one on this,

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:41.163
but it's nicer to get into the
detail with hand-polishing.

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:47.860
The keys have to be polished.

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:49.918
I put the rag in a vise

00:04:49.918 --> 00:04:51.743
and polish it from there

00:04:51.743 --> 00:04:55.490
and get into the corners
of every part of the key,

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:56.683
as far as you can.

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:02.916
After polishing, things will turn up

00:05:02.916 --> 00:05:03.810
that you didn't notice

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:07.150
when the horn was dirty and tarnished,

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:08.420
and you see slight ripples

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:10.250
because the polishing surface

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:12.480
will reflect the light much more.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:15.700
And here I'm smoothing
out little imperfections

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:16.725
with a hammer.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.000
Here, I use a hand-roller burnisher again

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.810
against a steel sickle that is
fitted to the table surface.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.680
This sickle will fit the rim of the bell,

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:30.855
and I can roll out the
dents on the bell rim.

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:40.090
After straightening up the
dent underneath the thumb hook,

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:41.507
I fit the hook

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:44.173
and I paste it on the
underside with a paste,

00:05:44.173 --> 00:05:46.750
which is keeping the soft solder flux,

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:48.322
which keeps the area clean

00:05:48.322 --> 00:05:50.930
and helps the solder flow
once I apply the heat.

00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:57.310
After soldering the thumb hook on,

00:05:57.310 --> 00:06:00.860
there will be some tiny
excess solder on the sides,

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:03.501
which I remove with a polishing knife.

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:09.250
After finding the pad
that fits the right key,

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:11.385
I preliminary fit it to the key,

00:06:11.385 --> 00:06:13.230
and once I'm happy with the fit,

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:16.940
I heat up some shellac
and stick it onto the pad

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:19.420
in order to put it back under the key,

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:22.020
fit it again, and then heat it into place.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:23.880
With the shellac as a binder,

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:26.713
the pad will stay in place
and it will be a nice seal.

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:31.390
Once all the keys are fitted,

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:33.673
you start by putting it together.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:37.670
And there's a progression
in how you do this.

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:39.750
You start with the right-hand mechanism

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:41.810
and you work your way
through the instrument,

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:43.380
and it sort of follows logically

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:45.263
what comes first and what comes last.

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:54.970
Restoring a saxophone like
this is long and arduous,

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:56.950
but at the end it is very satisfying

00:06:56.950 --> 00:07:00.846
to make life anew for
a saxophone from 1927.

